===Etymology 1===
From {{inh|cy|cel-bry-pro|*tro}}, related to {{cog|xbm|tro}} and middle {{cog|kw|tro}}.<ref>{{R:cy:GPC|tro}}</ref>  The ultimate origin is unclear; sometimes said to be from {{der|cy|grc|Τροία|t=Troy}}, referring to the city's maze-like walls, but this could just be a similarity enforced by folk etymology.<ref>Biology and Human Affairs. (1975). United Kingdom: British Social Biology Council, p. 66</ref><ref>Lindsay, J. (1963). A Short History of Culture, from Prehistory to the Renaissance. United States: Citadel Press, p. 126</ref> It could instead be from corruptions of {{m|cy|troed|t=foot}},<ref>{{R:cel:EDPC|head=treget-|pages=389}}</ref> {{der|cy|la|torqueo|t=I turn}}, or {{cog|la|tropus}}/{{cog|grc|τρόπος|t=a turn}}.  Also compare {{cog|fr|troller|t=to stroll, drag, wander about}}.<ref>Worcester, J. E. (1910). Worcester's Academic Dictionary: A New Etymological Dictionary of the English Language. United States: Lippincott, p. 551</ref> More at [[w:Caerdroia#Etymology|Caerdroia]].

